MODIFICATIONS -- every vendored file, and how many guards it carries
===================================================================

Generated from the tree, and kept honest by the property it asserts.  Apply
this transform to any file below and you get FlashInfer v0.6.15, byte for
byte:

    1. strip every `// PIE:` marker, its continuation comments, the
       `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` under it and that directive's matching
       `#endif` -- keeping the guarded BODY, which is upstream's, and
       DISCARDING the `#else` branch, if any, which is ours;
    2. rewrite every `#include <pie/...>` back to the spelling upstream
       wrote, using the map in `tests/vendor_manifest.rs`.

Step 2 is new and is currently the identity: no vendored file has moved out
of `csrc/vendor/flashinfer/` yet, so no vendored file spells `<pie/...>` and
the map is empty.  It exists BEFORE the move rather than after it, because
the thing it protects is the thing a move destroys.  `csrc/` is being cut by
ROLE rather than by provenance -- a device-stdlib header belongs beside the
other device-stdlib headers whoever wrote it -- and the whole risk of that cut
is that vendoring quietly becomes a fork.  A file that moves must change its
`#include` lines, because upstream reaches its siblings by RELATIVE path
(`"../cp_async.cuh"`, `"../utils.cuh"`: 58 such directives, and not one
`<flashinfer/...>` among them).  So the move edits upstream's bytes, and the
only thing standing between "vendoring plus a mechanical transform" and "a
fork" is a transform that is written down and checked.  A version bump stays
re-fetch plus re-apply.

`grep -rn "// PIE:" csrc/vendor/flashinfer/` finds all 33 markers.

Columns: guards, added lines, file lines, file bytes.

`bytes` is the fourth column and the newest.  `lines` alone is blind to an
in-place edit that keeps the line count -- change a constant, reflow an
expression, and all three of the other columns are unmoved.  A byte count is
not a checksum and does not catch a length-preserving edit either, but it
catches everything that changes a file's LENGTH, which is nearly every real
drift, and unlike a checksum it is computed identically by `wc -c` and by
`str::len` so the two sides cannot disagree about the algorithm.  The residue
-- a length-preserving edit outside every guard -- is what the network-side
check covers; `.wiki/driver/new-horizon.md` §23.8 records it being run.

